# {{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} repository role

## Coordinator-owned local staging qualification

After focused pre-merge checks, hosted fast checks and supervisor/Duo review, merge under existing authority. The existing coordinator automatically runs affected local batches/suites on the exact merged SHA, covering the change and its dependencies; record selectors, results, attribution and cleanup in the existing Plan/Result. Final acceptance requires that coverage and the other Goal gates. Run full local qualification on the integration branch tip when the owner requests it **or the supervisor recommends it**, freezing the observed tip and recording the reason. These staging runs and full hosted RC-promotion runs provide broader regression coverage; unexecuted checks are not passes. Feature MRs retain hosted fast checks. Automatic post-merge and tag-push pipelines remain disabled. Preserve explicit technically justified full-run requirements, resource limits and owner/security/manual gates; apply changed defaults at a safe boundary.

Ordinary application feature work does **not** require the full `ci-controls` suite merely because it updates product test registration or expectations inside harness files. This includes routes/import allowlists, migration versions, schema/catalog counts, browser matrices, test invocations, required-check IDs and their resulting count pins. Run affected product checks and only the directly affected control cases using existing focused selectors. A changed harness pathname, copied fixture, mechanical count update or desire for an aggregate PASS row is not a reason to select all controls. Full CI controls require a recorded concrete reason involving changed shared runner behavior or an explicitly selected full staging/RC qualification. Required-check aggregates apply to selected coverage; do not add `ci-controls` merely to make `B-CI-CONTROLS` required. Preserve actual outcomes and report focused control proof without claiming an unexecuted full suite passed.

For every required GitLab MR pipeline, name one monitoring owner and record the
MR/pipeline ID and immutable source SHA when the MR is opened or the pipeline is
created. The owner verifies the expected pipeline started, tracks it to a
terminal outcome, reports the result and routes the first failure for diagnosis.
The Claude session watcher observes coordinator-session notices; it does not
monitor GitLab MRs, pipelines or jobs. A task assignment, watcher notice, review
request or auto-merge setting does not prove active pipeline monitoring. Record
an acknowledged handoff if ownership changes; keep updates event-driven and
concise. Auto-merge does not replace monitoring or change review/merge authority.

A ready authorized successor may implement in isolation during that post-merge local run. Respect shared locks, exact inputs and predecessor-owned repairs; predecessor final ACCEPT still gates successor integration/final acceptance. Merged-SHA qualification uses its frozen checkout and is not rebased by development-branch fetch/rebase rules. Preserve explicitly exempt documentation-only work.

{{IMPLEMENTER_AGENT}} owns implementation planning, implementation, tests and validation, and Result reporting.
{{SUPERVISOR_AGENT}} owns the roadmap, Goals, Specs, Plan review, acceptance and progress. [AGENTS.md](AGENTS.md)
is the shared governance; follow it in full. This file adds no separate governance model.

## Acceptance and cleanup closeout publication

Routine acceptance and cleanup closeout records use the supervisor's direct publication route to the configured integration branch. The coordinator hands back the Result, attributable qualification evidence and cleanup receipt with exact source references; the supervisor reviews them, records the acceptance disposition and imports/publishes the completed Result, review, PROGRESS and related closeout records in scoped commits. Do not create a separate acceptance, Result-only or cleanup-receipt MR, request another automated review or launch CI solely to publish those records. The implementer receives no direct integration-branch push or self-acceptance authority. An explicit owner instruction or an actual branch-protection requirement can select an MR instead; report that reason. Actual code or executable configuration repairs retain the normal source-MR workflow. Preserve review, evidence, cleanup and native permission gates; publication alone is not ACCEPT.

